WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.160
In the mid-1960s a computer scientist named&nbsp;
Joseph Weisenbaum created one of the first natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.280
language processing programs, called ELIZA.&nbsp;
The user would type conversational sentences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.560
and get responses in return--a system that today&nbsp;
we might call a "chatbot," like the ones you find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.200
on customer support websites. ELIZA used simple&nbsp;
pattern matching and substitution to give the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:27.120
illusion of comprehension. She was designed with&nbsp;
the personality of a psychotherapist, so that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.000
could reply with canned responses, like "Why do&nbsp;
you say that?" or "Is that important to you?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.600
Weisenbaum actually created ELIZA to demonstrate&nbsp;
how shallow computer language skills were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.160
so imagine his surprise that many users actually&nbsp;
believed she understood what they were saying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.120
I guess it's in our nature to anthropomorphize&nbsp;
things. After all, I've been calling a computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.560
program "she". ELIZA was one of the first programs&nbsp;
that could participate in the Turing Test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.080
a theoretical experiment proposed by computer&nbsp;
scientist Alan Turing about 15 years earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.120
as an assessment of artificial intelligence. An&nbsp;
evaluator would eavesdrop on a typed conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.960
between two participants: a human and a computer.&nbsp;
If the evaluator was unable to determine which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.160
which, the computer was said to have passed the&nbsp;
test. Half a century later, computer technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.600
has evolved exponentially. We all carry around&nbsp;
a natural language processor in our pockets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.800
vastly more advanced than ELIZA. We ask it to&nbsp;
give us directions, keep track of our schedules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.600
even turn our lights on and off. But is any of&nbsp;
this technology close to passing the Turing Test?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:39.840
Even if it is, are computers really talking&nbsp;
to us? Or are they still just faking it?

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.760
Not long after ELIZA, another computer scientist&nbsp;
named Terry Winograd developed his own natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.280
language processor called SHRDLU. Unlike ELIZA,&nbsp;
SHRDLU was supposed to actually understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.320
what it was talking about--so to speak. Its&nbsp;
comprehension was limited to a handful of simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:07.840
objects and actions, but users could instruct it&nbsp;
to rearrange the objects around a virtual room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.000
and then ask questions to verify that&nbsp;
it knew what it had accomplished.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.560
SHRDLU was one of the first successful&nbsp;
attempts to teach a computer grammar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.760
to put words into categories like nouns, verbs&nbsp;
and prepositions. To have rules about how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.960
could combine and assign meanings to various&nbsp;
combinations. The technology was popularized in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.080
the text adventure video games of the 1980s, which&nbsp;
allowed players to interact with the game world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:44.480
by typing simple verb-noun commands like "GET&nbsp;
SWORD," "GO EAST," or "OPEN DOOR." Half the fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:50.160
of these games was trying different combinations&nbsp;
to see just how... extensive the vocabulary was

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.520
But this technology did not advance as quickly as&nbsp;
some hoped, because programmers soon realized that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.720
they had to manually code all the rules of English&nbsp;
grammar, which are a lot more complicated than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.720
"open door." Even a simple rule like "add -ed to&nbsp;
make a verb past tense" has enough exceptions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:14.400
give a human ESL student a headache. Even a simple&nbsp;
sentence like "It's raining," would confound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:20.080
a computer because it couldn't proceed without&nbsp;
knowing what "it" is. Grammar is also dependent on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:26.000
the listener having at least some understanding of&nbsp;
how the world works. Consider these two sentences:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.080
Take the cap off the milk and pour it.&nbsp;
Take the sheet off the bed and fold it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.120
These seem like pretty simple instructions, but&nbsp;
that's only because we already know that milk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.000
pours and sheets fold. A computer without that&nbsp;
prior knowledge would have no way of knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.160
what the "it's" refer to. And how about these two&nbsp;
sentences: Sarah drew the dog with a pencil. Sarah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.280
drew the dog with a bone. Again, neither of these&nbsp;
sentences would give a human much trouble. But a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.760
computer couldn't be sure of the meanings unless&nbsp;
it already knew that dogs don't carry pencils,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:02.560
and that you can't draw with bones. So just&nbsp;
to get a computer to understand basic grammar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:08.000
you'd have to manually encode it with vast complex&nbsp;
rules of syntax and a broad understanding of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.800
thousands of different objects interact with each&nbsp;
other. It's no wonder that in the 1990s computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:18.240
scientists kind of gave up on so-called symbolic&nbsp;
language processing in favor of a new strategy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.960
statistical language processing. Instead of&nbsp;
trying to teach a computer the rules of language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.720
they developed algorithms that could analyze large&nbsp;
bodies of text, look for patterns, and then make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.920
guesses based on statistical probabilities. For&nbsp;
instance, when you ask Siri "What's the weather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:39.360
looking like today?" she doesn't bother parsing&nbsp;
your grammar. She simply homes in on keywords and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:44.080
guesses what you're looking for based on how&nbsp;
common the request is. She'd probably give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.440
the same answer if you said "whether today" or&nbsp;
even just "weather." Another application of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.920
technology is predictive text, which is what&nbsp;
your phone does when it tries to guess which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:59.120
word you're going to type next. For example,&nbsp;
if you type the word "good" the algorithm knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.400
from looking at thousands of pages of text that&nbsp;
the most likely word to follow is "morning." It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.920
doesn't know why or what those words mean--just&nbsp;
that it's a statistical probability. Believe it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.720
or not, there was a time when some thought this&nbsp;
was how human speech worked; That our brains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.760
picked words one by one in order, deciding what&nbsp;
word was most likely to follow the one before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:26.160
20th century linguists like Noam Chomsky have&nbsp;
shown that human grammar is far too complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.200
to be constructed this way. Take this sentence,&nbsp;
for instance: The fact that you went for a walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.600
this late at night without an umbrella even&nbsp;
after hearing the weather report on the radio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:42.720
this afternoon. You can probably tell that there's&nbsp;
something missing. That's because the opening few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.320
words obligate the speaker to finish the sentence&nbsp;
with a verb phrase, like "is unbelievable." Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.200
brain subconsciously remembers this commitment,&nbsp;
even though there are 23 words in between.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.160 --> 00:06:00.480
Language is full of such grammatical promises,&nbsp;
like either-ors or if-thens. A computer program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.080
that only considers one word at a time would&nbsp;
never be able to fulfill them. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.080
recent advancements in digital neural&nbsp;
networks are raising expectations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.920
what predictive text can achieve. In 2020,&nbsp;
the artificial intelligence company OpenAI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:20.480
released a beta version of one of the most&nbsp;
sophisticated natural language processors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:26.400
ever created, called GPT-3. To find out how&nbsp;
GPT-3's extraordinary language capabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:32.560
are achieved, I spoke with OpenAI technical&nbsp;
director Dr. Ashley Pilipiszyn. How is GPT-3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:39.120
different from previous NLPs? Yeah, so unlike most&nbsp;
AI systems which are designed for one use case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:46.320
GPT-3 and our API provides a general purpose text&nbsp;
in text out interface, and it allows users to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:53.360
it on virtually any English language task. Say&nbsp;
I have a piece of a legal document maybe an NDA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:07:00.480
and I would ask GPT-3 to summarize this legal&nbsp;
document like a second grader and GPT-3 would then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:04.960
be able to provide a couple sentences actually&nbsp;
compressing and making that legal document&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:10.000
into a much more understandable piece of&nbsp;
text. So our model actually doesn't have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:16.080
goal or objective other than predicting the next&nbsp;
word. Like most predictive text programs, GPT-3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:23.040
is trained by feeding it a large body of text for&nbsp;
analysis, known as a corpus. And GPT-3's corpus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:30.640
is enormous. Somewhere around 2 billion pages of&nbsp;
text, taken from wikipedia, digital books, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:36.080
a vast swath of the web. It analyzes this text,&nbsp;
using hundreds of billions of parameters looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:41.360
for probabilities. And because it does much of&nbsp;
it unsupervised, even its programmers don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:46.800
exactly what patterns it's finding in our human&nbsp;
speech. When GPT-3 is asked to complete a prompt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.920
it uses what it's learned to guess what should&nbsp;
come next. But where your phone guesses words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:57.760
GPT-3 guesses "tokens": four character&nbsp;
blocks of text including spaces and symbols.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:02.720
And can you tell us a little bit more about these&nbsp;
particular tokens? So you and I as humans, when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:09.360
see a sentence we see a specific set of words with&nbsp;
spaces in between, etc. When GPT-3 quote-unquote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:14.320
"sees," they actually are seeing tokens, which you&nbsp;
can think of actually like a jigsaw puzzle--that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:21.600
allows GPT-3 to process more text. It's really&nbsp;
trying to predict what the next token is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:26.320
going to be in a sentence, based on all of the&nbsp;
previous text it's seen before in that prompt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.680
Because of its longer memory it's able to complete&nbsp;
grammatical commitments like "the fact that..."

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.160
And thanks to its huge corpus,&nbsp;
it actually does seem to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:41.040
that dogs are more interested&nbsp;
in bones than pencils.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:56.240
It can even apply grammatical rules to unfamiliar&nbsp;
words. A famous experiment from the 1950s asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.200
children to complete the following prompt. Even&nbsp;
though they had never seen the word "wug" before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.040
the vast majority of children were able&nbsp;
to correctly apply an "-s" to make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:10.880
plural. Similarly, even though GPT-3 has&nbsp;
probably never seen the word "ferfumfer"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:17.120
in all its billions of pages of corpus, it still&nbsp;
knows to add an "-s" if you have two of them. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:23.360
can GPT-3 pass the Turing Test? Uh, most people&nbsp;
familiar with the traditional Turing Test would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:28.960
probably say it comes very close. It can actually&nbsp;
start to feel like you really are interacting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:35.040
you know, with that person. But the longer&nbsp;
you talk with it and really begin to push it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:40.880
you do come across some mistakes. And so that does&nbsp;
kind of indicate that okay yeah this isn't human.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:48.560
So ultimately, it's it's coming close but it's&nbsp;
not quite there. Despite GPT-3's often impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:54.960
performance, there is a fundamental difference&nbsp;
between human speech and what GPT-3 does. Humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:02.000
don't learn language by memorizing likely orders&nbsp;
of words, but instead word categories. Chomsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:07.680
demonstrated this with a famously nonsensical&nbsp;
sentence: "Colorless green ideas sleep furiously."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:13.440
Even though all your life you've probably never&nbsp;
heard any of these words follow the one before it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.560
you still know that the sentence is grammatically&nbsp;
correct, because the order of the word categories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:24.240
is correct. But GPT-3 does not make a&nbsp;
distinction between form and content.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.880
It doesn't care that "colorless green ideas"&nbsp;
is a grammatically correct noun phrase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:34.560
Only that the likelihood of those tokens&nbsp;
going together is very, very small. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:39.200
why a slightly more advanced question from&nbsp;
the wug test can get... interesting results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:47.440
Our brains seem to be hardwired for grammar,&nbsp;
which ironically is closer to how the old SHRDLU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:53.360
program worked. We have thoughts about the world&nbsp;
that exist prior to and independent of language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:59.120
and we use grammar as the container to deliver&nbsp;
those thoughts to others. For all its complexity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:04.160
GPT-3 is still guessing one token at a&nbsp;
time, based on statistical probabilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:08.880
It has no plan or goal, unless given one&nbsp;
by a human. That's why the developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:13.840
at OpenAI prefer to think of GPT-3 as a&nbsp;
writer's tool rather than a writer itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:18.240
It's pretty astounding at faking&nbsp;
what human speech sounds like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.480
but humans are still the only computers that&nbsp;
can create the thoughts behind the words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:29.920
A big thanks to Dr. Ashley Pilipiszyn from OpenAI,&nbsp;
and many of the responses shown in this episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:35.360
were obtained through AI Dungeon, a procedurally&nbsp;
generated text adventure powered by GPT-3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:41.440
You can check it out yourself&nbsp;
through the link in the description.

